# UI copy is sentence case

Every string a person reads in a Podverse app — web, management-web, mobile, and the transactional
emails the API sends — is **sentence case**: capitalize the first character and nothing else except
words that are capitalized in running prose anyway.

```text
GOOD  Add feed · Create playlist · Mark as played · Sleep timer · Save changes · Sign up
BAD   Add Feed · Create Playlist · Mark as Played · Sleep Timer · Save Changes · Sign Up
```

This holds for **every** kind of copy, at every size: buttons, screen and section titles, table
headers, form labels and placeholders, menu items, modal titles, toasts, empty states, error and
success messages, accessibility labels (`aria-label` / `accessibilityLabel`), page titles and SEO
metadata, and email subjects and button text — **except** the standing exceptions listed below
(today: English mobile bottom tab labels).

One standard, all surfaces. iOS convention is Title Case for buttons and Android/Material is
sentence case, and Podverse ships one catalog to both — so the platform argument cannot settle it,
and following each platform would mean the same key reading two ways. Sentence case is also what the
large majority of the catalog already used before it was normalized, and it is what Spanish, French,
and Greek do natively, so translations do not have to fight the source.

## What stays capitalized

Proper nouns keep their own capitalization wherever they appear, mid-string included:

- **Brands and products:** Podverse, Podcast Index, Metaboost, Lightning, Bitcoin, Alby, Nostr,
  PayPal, Stripe, Apple, Google, F-Droid, CarPlay, Android Auto, Discord, Mastodon, Matrix, Next.js,
  Buy Me a Coffee, Unified Push, Web Push.
- **Acronyms and initialisms:** RSS, OPML, URL, ID, API, HTML, V4V, LNURL, FOSS, GB, USD.
- **Membership tier names:** Premium, Trial (`Buy Premium membership`, `Your Trial status has …`).
- **Taxonomy category names** (`categories.*`): Arts, Health & Fitness, Kids & Family, Science
  Fiction — these mirror the directory taxonomy, not our copy.
- **Keyboard key names** (`settings.keyboard.*`): Space, Arrow Left, Enter, Escape.
- **A UI element quoted by name inside a sentence**, when lowercasing it would hide which control is
  meant: `press the "Add feed" button`, `choose None to omit it`, `Screens you hide stay in More`.
  The quoted label itself still follows sentence case — only its status as a name survives.

Legal documents under `apps/api/legal/**` are authored prose, not UI copy; their own headings and
capitalization are out of scope.

## Where this applies

- `packages/i18n-catalog/*/originals/en-US.json` — the authoring source. Get it right here and every
  surface inherits it.
- `packages/i18n-catalog/*/originals/{es,fr,el-GR}.json` and `overrides/*` — each language's own
  sentence-case convention (all three capitalize only the first word and proper nouns). LLM-generated
  translations tend to mirror English Title Case; fix that when you touch them.
- Hardcoded copy that has not moved into the catalog yet (placeholder pages, API email
  `buttonText`, `packages/ui` fallback strings). Casing applies even while the string is a literal —
  see **i18n-user-facing-strings** for getting it into the catalog.
- E2E text assertions (`getByRole({ name })`, `getByText`, Maestro `text:`) must match the shipped
  copy exactly. Playwright's default name matching is case-insensitive, so a stale assertion can
  pass while lying about the product — update it anyway.

Route and tab **identifiers** (`'My Library'` in `tabLayout.ts` / navigation param lists) are code,
not copy. Leave them; the visible label comes from the catalog.

## Standing exceptions (grow this list)

Sentence case remains the default. When the operator confirms a durable Title Case (or other)
exception, add it here and keep the catalog string matching that exception. Do not invent new
exceptions; only list ones the operator has confirmed.

| Surface | Exception | Example |
| ------- | --------- | ------- |
| Mobile bottom tab bar labels (`nav.tab.*` / `tabBarLabel`) | **Title Case** in English | `My Library` (not `My library`) |

Stack titles, More hub rows, web sidebar, buttons, and every other surface stay sentence case unless
listed above. Prefer a **mobile-only** catalog key under `nav.tab.*` when the same concept must stay
sentence case on web (`features.my_library` → `My library` on web; `nav.tab.my_library` →
`My Library` on the mobile tab).

## When the operator asks for Title Case

If a request would introduce Title Case — a new string, a rename, a "match the old app" ask, or a
direct instruction to capitalize a label — **stop and ask the operator to confirm** before writing
it. Name the rule and what the sentence-case version would be, and let them decide. Do not silently
comply, and do not silently refuse and file it as sentence case: a one-off exception is theirs to
make, not yours to assume either way.

If they confirm it as a **standing** exception (not a one-off), add it to the table above and use the
confirmed casing going forward for that surface. Confirmation of a one-off covers that request only.
